\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Eliminating alternate vertices of a ring-valued cycle}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{Use the [four-cycle endpoints](fdip-0003)
#{s(e_i)=i}, #{h(e_i)=i+1} on #{\mathbb Z/4\mathbb Z}, but now
let #{R} be any commutative unital ring and take #{t,\mu\in R}.
Give successive edges weights #{(1,t,1,t)}. On the finite function
modules with their [counting pairings](fdip-001F), set
##{Q=d^\dagger Wd+\mu I,\qquad s=1+t+\mu.}
Here #{d} is [incidence](fdip-0007) and #{W} multiplies each edge
field by its weight. This extends the positive-real weight choice
in \ref{fdip-0003}; no weight is assumed positive or invertible.}

\p{First compute from endpoints. The difference is
#{(df)(e_i)=f(i+1)-f(i)}. Grouping the incoming and outgoing edge
terms gives
##{(d^\dagger Wdf)(i)
 =w(e_{i-1})(f(i)-f(i-1))+w(e_i)(f(i)-f(i+1)).}
Adding #{\mu f(i)} yields
##{\begin{aligned}
(Qf)(0)&=sf(0)-f(1)-tf(3),\\
(Qf)(1)&=sf(1)-f(0)-tf(2),\\
(Qf)(2)&=sf(2)-tf(1)-f(3),\\
(Qf)(3)&=sf(3)-tf(0)-f(2).
\end{aligned}}}

\p{Choose the additional [partition](fdip-001I)
#{V_0=\{0,2\}}, #{V_1=\{1,3\}}, and put
#{X=\mathcal F_R(V_0)}, #{Y=\mathcal F_R(V_1)}.
Restriction and extension by zero give the chosen splitting.
The resulting [component maps](fdip-001T) are
##{\begin{aligned}
A&=sI_X,&C&=sI_Y,\\
(Ex)(1)&=-x(0)-tx(2),&(Ex)(3)&=-tx(0)-x(2),\\
(By)(0)&=-y(1)-ty(3),&(By)(2)&=-ty(1)-y(3).
\end{aligned}}
The pivot #{C} is invertible exactly when #{s} is a unit.
A unit gives #{C^{-1}=s^{-1}I_Y}. Conversely, if #{CJ=I_Y},
evaluation of #{CJ\delta_1=\delta_1} at vertex #{1} gives
#{s(J\delta_1)(1)=1}; commutativity makes this a two-sided inverse
of #{s}. In the zero ring #{0=1} is a unit, consistently with this
argument.}

\p{Assume this unit condition. Applying #{B} to #{Ex} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
(BEx)(0)&=(1+t^2)x(0)+2tx(2),\\
(BEx)(2)&=2tx(0)+(1+t^2)x(2).
\end{aligned}}
Consequently the split Schur map is
##{\begin{aligned}
(Sx)(0)&=[s-s^{-1}(1+t^2)]x(0)-2ts^{-1}x(2),\\
(Sx)(2)&=[s-s^{-1}(1+t^2)]x(2)-2ts^{-1}x(0).
\end{aligned}}
Here #{2=1+1}; neither #{2}, #{t}, #{\mu} nor #{S} is asserted to
be invertible. In coordinates ordered #{(0,2)}, the same map is
##{[S]=\begin{pmatrix}
s-s^{-1}(1+t^2)&-2ts^{-1}\\
-2ts^{-1}&s-s^{-1}(1+t^2)
\end{pmatrix}.}
This array presents the map just computed from endpoints.}

\p{The [half-turn](fdip-0003) exchanges #{0\leftrightarrow2} and
#{1\leftrightarrow3}. Let #{r_X,r_Y} exchange the corresponding
field values. The component formulas give
#{Ar_X=r_XA}, #{Cr_Y=r_YC}, #{Er_X=r_YE} and #{Br_Y=r_XB};
hence #{Sr_X=r_XS}. A one-step shift exchanges the two parts and
fails to preserve the alternating weights when #{t\ne1}.
No retained partition is implicit in general incidence data.}

\p{Now specialize to #{R=\mathbb R}, #{t>0}, #{\mu=m^2>0}.
The [weighted energy identity](fdip-0008) gives, for #{f\ne0},
##{b(f,Qf)=\sum_e w(e)(df(e))^2+m^2\sum_v f(v)^2>0.}
Thus #{Q} is positive definite and invertible, and the
[positive-real criterion](fdip-001Y) gives #{S>0}.
At #{t=m=1}, #{s=3} and
##{[S]=\frac13\begin{pmatrix}7&-2\\-2&7\end{pmatrix}.}
To solve #{Sx=\delta_2}, solve
#{7x(0)-2x(2)=0} and #{-2x(0)+7x(2)=3}.
These give #{x(2)=7/15} and #{x(0)=2/15}. Therefore
##{(\pi_XQ^{-1}\iota_X\delta_2)(0)
 =(S^{-1}\delta_2)(0)=\frac{2}{15}.}
By contrast, fixing the eliminated field to zero leaves the retained
map #{A=3I_X}, and #{(A^{-1}\delta_2)(0)=0}. These are different
map operations.}

\p{At real #{\mu=0,t>0}, the pivot #{C=(1+t)I_Y} is still
invertible. But
#{s-s^{-1}(1+t^2)=2t/(1+t)}, so the nonzero constant retained
field satisfies #{S1=0}. The [kernel correspondence](fdip-001W)
then recovers the constant full field in #{\ker Q}, since
#{-C^{-1}E1=1}.
An invertible pivot does not imply an invertible full precision.}

\p{\citet{Section 2.1, pp. 4-5}{spielman2009effective} gives the
incidence sign convention for positive real weights. The arbitrary-ring
identities use the direct counting construction of \ref{fdip-0007}.}

\p{\citet{Appendix C.4.1, pp. 672-673}{boyd2004convex} gives the real
elimination convention. All coefficients and inverse entries here were computed from the
specified endpoints and weights. They recover the algebraic values
in [real alternate-vertex elimination](fdip-000F); the covariance and
marginal-law interpretation there additionally uses the Gaussian
measure. No massless Gaussian formula follows by substituting
#{\mu=0} into a massive normalization.}
